## Reviewer
### Summary

This paper studies the problem of intra-modal misalignment in contrastive pre-trained vision-language models, such as CLIP. The authors argue that the common practice of individually exploiting the text or image encoders of these powerful multimodal models is highly suboptimal for intra-modal tasks like image-to-image retrieval. To address this, the authors propose to transform intra-modal tasks to inter-modal ones via modality inversion to exploit CLIP’s inter-modal alignment. Extensive experiments are conducted to demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method.

### Soundness

3 good
### Presentation

3 good
### Contribution

2 fair
### Strengths

1. The paper is well-written and easy to follow.
2. The proposed method is simple and effective.
3. The paper conducts extensive experiments to demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method.

### Weaknesses

1. The novelty of the proposed method is limited. The idea of modality inversion has been explored in previous works, such as [1]. The authors should discuss the differences between the proposed method and previous works.
2. The authors only conduct experiments on image-to-image and text-to-text retrieval tasks. It would be better to conduct experiments on other tasks, such as zero-shot image classification.
3. The authors should provide more analysis on the proposed method. For example, how does the number of pseudo-word tokens and optimization steps affect the performance?
4. The authors should provide more details about the datasets used in the experiments, such as the number of samples, the size of the gallery, etc.

[1] Li et al., "Text2Image: Textual Inversion for Text-to-Image Diffusion Models", CVPR 2023.

### Questions

Please see the weakness above.

