## Reviewer

### Summary

This paper studies the modality gap between text and image feature embedding spaces in pre-trained multi-modal models. The authors show that the common practice of individually exploiting the text or image encoders of these powerful multimodal models is highly suboptimal for intra-modal tasks like image-to-image retrieval. They propose to transform native modality inputs into inter-modal representations using two optimization-based modality inversion techniques and the inductive bias of the pre-trained encoder of the complementary modality. They show that this approach improves performance on multiple tasks and datasets.

### Soundness

3 good
### Presentation

3 good
### Contribution

3 good
### Strengths

- The paper is well-written and easy to follow.
- The proposed method is simple and effective.
- The paper conducts extensive experiments to demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method.

### Weaknesses

- The paper only focuses on the CLIP model and does not consider other multi-modal models.
- The paper does not provide a detailed analysis of the computational cost of the proposed method.

### Questions

- The paper mentions that the proposed method is computationally expensive. Could you provide a more detailed analysis of the computational cost of the proposed method?
- The paper only focuses on the CLIP model. Could you provide results on other multi-modal models such as BLIP-2 [1] and VLM-BFF [2]?

[1] https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.06572
[2] https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14289

